feat(exploits): add recon, exploit probes and post-exploitation command pack

Everything here was tested against the real lab target 192.168.1.46
(Ministry of Pony, Apache 2.4.20 / PHP 7.3.19 / Drupal 7.20 /
Coppermine 1.4.14). The recorded results matter more than the tools:
both CMS paths turned out to be patched.

Tools:
- fingerprint.py      identify CMS versions, exposed files, form shape
- recon.sh            port scan, banners, directory exposure
- drupalgeddon.py     SA-CORE-2014-005 SQL injection
- check-sql-errors.py look for SQL error text to prove/disprove injection
- drupalgeddon2.py    CVE-2018-7600 pre-auth RCE
- verify-rce.py       decisive RCE test (distinguishes execution from
                      form reflection, which caused a false positive)
- cve-2018-7600-variants.py  five payload shapes, reports JSON vs HTML
- check-coppermine.py probe Coppermine 1.4.14 entry points
- lab-shell.sh        in-VM environment self-check
- post-exploitation.sh   eight-stage read-only collection pack

Findings (see exploits/README.md):
- Drupal SQLi: not injectable. 1=1 and 1=2 give identical responses, six
  error payloads produce no SQL text, sleep(5) returns in 0.03s.
- Drupal RCE: not reachable. All five variants return a full HTML page
  instead of the AJAX JSON that a genuine hit produces.
- Coppermine: every entry script returns HTTP 500 because 2007-era PHP 5
  code cannot run on PHP 7.3.

Lesson captured in the docs: a version banner is a lead, not a verdict.
Verify each candidate with a decisive oracle before building a story on it.
